class Solution {
    public ListNode insertBeforeHead(ListNode head, int data) {

        // Create a new node
        ListNode newnode = new ListNode(data);

        // Connect new node to the existing head
        newnode.next = head;

        // Update prev pointer only if the list is not empty
        if (head != null) {
            head.prev = newnode;
        }

        // Return the new head
        return newnode;
    }
}

1. Why don't we check before updating newnode.next?
newnode.next = head;


Here:
- newnode is a newly created node.
- It will never be null because we just created it.
- head is allowed to be null.
Assigning null to a reference is perfectly valid in Java.

So even if head == null, this works:
newnode.next = null;


2. Why do we check before updating head.prev?
head.prev = newnode;


Here, we are accessing the prev field of head.

If head == null, Java cannot access prev because there is no node.

That's why we write:
if (head != null) {
    head.prev = newnode;
}
